## Predefined Macros Reference

SubC automatically defines the following macros based on the target:

### Always Defined
- `__SUBC__` - Always `1`

### OS Macros (from `cg_os_config`)
| OS | Macros |
|----|--------|
| Linux | `__linux__`, `__linux`, `__unix__`, `__unix`, `__ELF__` |
| FreeBSD | `__FreeBSD__`, `__unix__`, `__unix`, `__ELF__` |
| NetBSD | `__NetBSD__`, `__unix__`, `__unix`, `__ELF__` |
| OpenBSD | `__OpenBSD__`, `__unix__`, `__unix`, `__ELF__` |
| Darwin | `__APPLE__`, `__MACH__`, `__unix__`, `__unix` |
| Windows | `_WIN32`, `__WIN32__`, `__WINDOWS__` |
| DOS | `__DOS__`, `__dos`, `MSDOS` |
| MVS | `__MVS__`, `__IBM__`, `__EBCDIC__` |

### Architecture Macros (from `cg_arch`)
| Arch | Macros |
|------|--------|
| i386 | `__i386__`, `__i386` |
| x86-64 | `__x86_64__`, `__x86_64`, `__amd64__`, `__amd64` |
| AArch64 | `__aarch64__`, `__arm64__` |
| ARM | `__arm__`, `__ARM_ARCH` |
| 8086 | `__8086__`, `__i8086__` |

### Type Size Macros (generated from `cg_arch` values)
- `__SIZEOF_CHAR__` - Always 1
- `__SIZEOF_SHORT__` - 2 on all targets
- `__SIZEOF_INT__` - 2 (8086), 4 (i386/ARM), 8 (x86-64/AArch64)
- `__SIZEOF_LONG__` - 4 (8086/i386/ARM), 8 (x86-64/AArch64)
- `__SIZEOF_POINTER__` - 2 (8086), 4 (i386/ARM), 8 (x86-64/AArch64)
- `__SIZEOF_FLOAT__` - 4 on all targets
- `__SIZEOF_DOUBLE__` - 8 on all targets
- `__SIZEOF_SIZE_T__` - Same as `__SIZEOF_POINTER__`
- `__SIZEOF_PTRDIFF_T__` - Same as `__SIZEOF_POINTER__`

### Endianness Macros
- `__LITTLE_ENDIAN__` - Defined if little-endian (all current targets)
- `__BIG_ENDIAN__` - Defined if big-endian
- `__BYTE_ORDER__` - 1234 (little) or 4321 (big)
- `__ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__` - Always 1234
- `__ORDER_BIG_ENDIAN__` - Always 4321

### Data Model Macros
- `__LP64__` - Defined if `long` and `pointer` are 64-bit
- `__ILP32__` - Defined if `int`, `long`, and `pointer` are 32-bit

### Override via -D

All predefined macros can be overridden using the `-D` flag:

```bash
# Override __SIZEOF_INT__ for testing
scc -D __SIZEOF_INT__=4 -T darwin-aarch64 test.c
```
